Tests that need a running cluster
=================================

These drive real clusters: they create publications, slots and
subscriptions, run a workload, kill things, and compare the two sides.
They are not part of "make check" and print their own verdict; each one
exits non-zero when it fails, so they can be chained.

They expect psql and, where they reconfigure a cluster, the gp tools on
PATH; each sources ~/cloudberry-db-devel/cloudberry-env.sh if it is
there, and otherwise relies on the environment it is run in.

The module must be preloaded on the target cluster and its catalog
objects must exist there (see ../../README).  The source cluster needs
wal_level = logical and nothing else.  An existing cluster that predates
this module has no catalog objects, because they go in at initdb;
install them by hand with

    psql -d <database> -f \
      $GPHOME/share/postgresql/cdb_init.d/mpp_logicalrep_cdbinit.sql


One cluster
-----------

Everything that replicates between two databases of one cluster now runs
under the tree's own test frameworks, against whatever cluster
"make installcheck" is pointed at:

    ../../sql/                 pg_regress: the subscription DDL contract
                               and who may read what
    ../../isolation2/sql/      isolation2: replication end to end, the
                               initial synchronisation, a table added to
                               a running subscription, the statistics
                               views, fault injection under load, and the
                               copy transport on its own

    cd contrib/mpp_logicalrep && make installcheck installcheck-isolation2

What is left here needs something SQL cannot reach -- the helper binary
driven by hand, file modes on disk, or a measurement rather than a
verdict:

    copy_transport.sh       the 2 -> N case of the transport, where a
                            target segment expects no client at all and
                            the sends are driven from a shell pipe.  The
                            N -> N and 2N -> N cases are in
                            isolation2/sql/copy_transport.sql
    security.sh             that the source's password stays out of the
                            dispatched payload, and that the copy
                            transport's socket drops a caller which does
                            not know the job name, with the port and done
                            files owner-only.  Who may read the
                            connection string and drive the entry points
                            is in sql/security.sql
    participants_bench.sh   how long small distributed transactions take
                            to arrive (a measurement, not a pass/fail
                            test)

    usage: <test>.sh [-s <port>]        (default 7100)


Two clusters
------------

These need a second cluster as the target, which is what the module is
actually for: the two sides then have their own segments, their own
distributed transactions and their own failures.

    end_to_end.sh           the whole module -- initial synchronisation
                            of a mixed schema under load, streaming,
                            four kinds of fault injected while the
                            workload runs, a table added to the running
                            subscription, and a comparison per table and
                            per segment at the end.  The one to run
                            first, and the one to run after a change.
    distribution_keys.sh    the target is distributed on something other
                            than the source's key: which shapes work,
                            which are refused, which are only warned
                            about, and that an error which cannot pass
                            disables the subscription
    slot_loss.sh            the source's slots disappear under a running
                            subscription, as a segment failover does

    usage: <test>.sh [-s <source port>] [-t <target port>]
           (defaults: source 7100, target 7300)


A target cluster
----------------

Any cluster will do; a demo one is made from the source tree with

    cd ~/workspace/cbdb-target &&
    COORDINATOR_DATADIR=$PWD PORT_BASE=7300 NUM_PRIMARY_MIRROR_PAIRS=3 \
    WITH_MIRRORS=false ~/workspace/cbdb/gpAux/gpdemo/demo_cluster.sh

Run it from the target's own directory: demo_cluster.sh writes
gpdemo-env.sh into the directory it is run in, and would otherwise
overwrite the source cluster's.

The number of segments need not match the source's, and that difference
is worth running in both directions -- a target with fewer segments than
the source, and one with more.
